    "> In SDL3, you achieve rotation and stretching (scaling) simultaneously using the SDL_RenderTextureRotated function.The primary cause of unwanted stretching or distortion during rotation is confusing how the destination rectangle (dstrect) behaves. In SDL3, dstrect defines the unrotated dimensions and location. The rotation angle is then applied to that box dynamically by the GPU.Standard GPU-Accelerated ApproachTo stretch a texture to custom dimensions and rotate it, define the desired visual width and height in the dstrect structure. Do not swap the width and height yourself when the angle changes, or the image will squash and distort.c// Define the source area (NULL uses the entire texture)> \nSDL_FRect srcrect = { 0.0f, 0.0f, texture_width, texture_height };> \n> \n// Define the target layout. Adjust 'w' and 'h' to stretch the texture.> \nSDL_FRect dstrect = { x_position, y_position, desired_width, desired_height };> \n> \n// Rotation configuration> \ndouble angle = 45.0; // In degrees, clockwise rotation> \nSDL_FPoint center = { desired_width / 2.0f, desired_height / 2.0f }; // Pivot point> \n> \n// Render, stretch, and rotate simultaneously> \nSDL_RenderTextureRotated(renderer, texture, &srcrect, &dstrect, angle, &center, SDL_FLIP_NONE);> \nUse code with caution.Key Parameters Breakdownsrcrect: Controls what part of the texture is sampled. Set to NULL to sample the whole asset.dstrect: Explicitly controls stretching. If desired_width and desired_height do not match the aspect ratio of your source texture, the texture stretches to fill this boundary before the rotation angle takes effect.center: The pivot point for rotation, relative to the top-left corner of dstrect. Set to NULL to default exactly to the center of the rectangle (w/2, h/2).Quality Control: Linear FilteringStretching and rotating can cause jagged pixel artifacts. Set the scaling hint or texture properties before rendering to enable smooth linear interpolation:cSDL_SetTextureScaleMode(texture, SDL_SCALEMODE_LINEAR);> \nUse code with caution.CPU-Side Alternative (Surfaces)If you are working with software surfaces instead of hardware-accelerated textures, use SDL_RotateSurface paired with SDL_StretchSurface. Be aware that this method runs on the CPU and will be significantly slower than SDL_RenderTextureRotated.If you are experiencing a specific type of image distortion, let me know what behavior you are seeing (e.g., aspect ratio breaking, unexpected clipping) or if you need to calculate rotated bounding boxes for collision detection.",
